From cf17346fde2b81ab64cbccd243b92c98be9b3863 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work8_fin_tc <adbtrr_work8_fin_tc@local>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 06:18:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] os/kernel/binary_manager: Add ASSERT hitting test case
 for recover_tcb

While recovering a faulted binary, binary_manager_recover_tcb() releases
every resource its threads were blocked on. A thread parked in
TSTATE_WAIT_SEM always carries a valid waitsem whose count went negative
when it blocked, so the function guards the release with
ASSERT(sem != NULL && sem->semcount < 0).

binary_manager_recover_tcb() is static, so add
binary_manager_recover_tcb_asserttest() next to it, built only when the
test case is enabled. It drives the recovery with a thread that reports
TSTATE_WAIT_SEM while carrying no wait semaphore, which is the corrupted
bookkeeping the assertion is there to catch.

 - os/kernel/binary_manager/binary_manager_recovery.c : test only entry
   point for the static recovery helper.
 - os/drivers/os_api_test/kernel/test_assert_binary_manager.c : kernel
   side helper reached through the TESTIOC_ASSERT_BINARY_MANAGER ioctl.
 - apps/examples/testcase/le_tc/kernel/tc_assert_binary_manager.c : user
   side test case. A return from the ioctl is a test case failure.

Controlled by CONFIG_TC_KERNEL_ASSERT_BINARY_MANAGER, default n, depends
on CONFIG_BINMGR_RECOVERY. The assertion is an ASSERT(), so CONFIG_DEBUG
is not required. It crashes the board by design and must be enabled alone.
